# Battle interruption lines: each interruption gets its own ~ titled section below,
# called directly by title (not chained through a single ~ start) since these are
# meant to be triggered independently from combat code as one-off interjections.

~ vorkoth_to_astryn
vorkoth_p1: Still standing? How cute.
=> END

~ vorkoth_to_novius
vorkoth_p1: Guilt looks good on you. Still trying to make up for all the people you turned away?
=> END

~ vorkoth_to_spero
vorkoth_p1: Watch the temper, kid. Wouldn't want your home to get raided again.
spero: *grinning* Says the guy who's been "raided" by his own forehead.
vorkoth_p1: ...Excuse me?
spero: Keep swinging, big guy.
=> END

~ vorkoth_to_aegrandir
vorkoth_p1: Old man should be tending a garden, not playing soldier.
=> END

~ vorkoth_to_astryn_stage2_1
vorkoth_p2: A woman giving orders on my battlefield. How novel.
=> END

~ vorkoth_to_spero_stage2
vorkoth_p2: Your people should be grateful I let you come in MY city at all.
spero: Say that again once you're on the ground.
vorkoth_p2: Still can't keep that mouth shut, can you, kid?
spero: Nah. Especially not when your forehead's doing all the talking for me.
vorkoth_p2: I WILL END YOU FIRST.
novius: *chuckles* You really made him say that with a straight face.
spero: Worth it.
=> END

~ vorkoth_to_aegrandir_stage2
vorkoth_p2: Ancient and useless. Your people should've died out generations ago.
aegrandir: And yet... here I still am.
=> END

~ vorkoth_to_novius_stage2
vorkoth_p2: Keep playing doctor. Won't matter when you're all in the ground.
=> END

~ vorkoth_to_crowd_stage2
vorkoth_p2: YOU SEE WHAT RESPONSIBILITIES I'VE HAD TO CARRY, KEEPING ANIMALS LIKE THIS IN LINE?
npcgreen: They aren't what you've been carrying. They represent what you've been TAKING from us!
npcblue: Defeat him! Don't let him walk away!
=> END

~ vorkoth_to_astryn_stage2_2
vorkoth_p2: No man in this city would let a woman lead him into battle. Yet here you are, playing general.
=> END

~ vorkoth_to_astryn_stage2_3
vorkoth_p2: Pathetic, really, that this is what passes for a leader nowadays.
=> END